  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis show a mid level ridge \nbuilding into the Great Lakes with sfc high pressure just downstream \nof it over the Ontario/Quebec province line and the east coast. A \ndeep trough is noted upstream, pivoting south over the Canadian \nPrairie. A low pressure lifting to the northwest of the CWA through \nSaturday morning tightens the pressure gradient, strengthening \nsoutherly flow and yielding a breezy period through Saturday. WAA \nkeeps temps ridiculously warm for November 20th/21st (20F to 30F \nabove normal) with highs this afternoon in the 50s and even warmer \ntemps on Saturday in the mid 50s to low 60s! Overnight temps bottom \nout in the 50s, staying near 60 in the far west where there is \nstrong downslope flow. This stable environment tonight into Saturday \nmorning yields a tricky forecast with regards to how strong gusts \nwill be. Widespread gusts are expected in the 20-30 mph realm, but \nareas in the higher terrain and just downstream are looking more \nbetween 30-35 mph. Gusts up to 45 mph are possible (40-60%) in the \nfar northern portions of Baraga and Marquette counties toward Lake \nSuperior and model soundings suggest isolated gusts of similar \nmagnitude are possible (~20-40% chance) in other areas of the west \nand northern tier.\n\nThe aforementioned low sends a cold front across the UP Saturday \nevening into Sunday morning. PWATs rise to around 1 inch, yielding \n0.25 to 0.75 inches of rain by the end of Sunday when associated \nshowers finally have pushed through. Probabilities of exceeding 1 \ninch are the same as last night (30-60%), but have trended westward \nand are highest over Marquette/Iron/Delta counties. Temps fall \nsteadily Saturday night through Sunday from the 50s toward the 30s. \n\nLake effect precip kicks off Sunday evening as 850 mb temps drop \ninto the negative single digits, continuing down to -11C to -15C by \nthe end of Monday. A potentially interesting interaction of a \ntrailing wave and sfc trough with a low-level convergence signature \ncould focus a heavier band of precip across a portion of the western \nUP/Keweenaw/north-central Sunday night/Monday, although it would \nhave to overcome spotty thermodynamics. Lake effect snow in the \nnorth to northwest wind belts is expected, yielding 1 to 2 inches of \nsnow by Monday night. There is a 30-50% chance for up to 4 inches. \nLake effect precip and cloud cover, onshore flow off a warmer Lake \nSuperior and a boundary layer that does not decouple overnight \nmoderates temps Sunday night. Warmed overnight temps from the NBM, \nkeeping lows in the mid 20s to low 30s. This colder airmass brings \nback seasonable temps in the 30s to low 40s next week, lows drop to \nteens and 20s. High pressure and dry weather through the \nremainder of the work week.",
